WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.835 --> 00:00:01.961
- [Narrator] Games.

00:00:01.961 --> 00:00:04.861
We've been playing them forever.

00:00:04.861 --> 00:00:05.694
But why?

00:00:06.587 --> 00:00:07.447
- Games are fun.

00:00:07.447 --> 00:00:08.382
- [Narrator] Okay.

00:00:08.382 --> 00:00:10.087
But, what else?

00:00:10.087 --> 00:00:12.520
- Nature takes play very seriously.

00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:16.298
Play offers safe practice
for learning the skills

00:00:16.298 --> 00:00:19.299
you need to do things
that in nature may even

00:00:19.299 --> 00:00:21.264
relate to your survival.

00:00:21.264 --> 00:00:24.097
(whimsical music)

00:00:26.708 --> 00:00:28.450
- [Narrator] This is Matt.

00:00:28.450 --> 00:00:29.406
- Oh, hi.

00:00:29.406 --> 00:00:31.451
I'm Matt Miller, and I think you should

00:00:31.451 --> 00:00:33.118
ditch that textbook.

00:00:35.884 --> 00:00:39.212
- [Narrator] And this is Joe and Kama.

00:00:39.212 --> 00:00:40.045
Guys?

00:00:41.580 --> 00:00:42.592
(clears throat)

00:00:42.592 --> 00:00:44.258
- You ready? Okay.

00:00:44.258 --> 00:00:45.612
- My name is Kama Tothero.

00:00:45.612 --> 00:00:47.243
- And my name is Joe Tothero.

00:00:47.243 --> 00:00:49.356
- We are avid game enthusiasts.

00:00:49.356 --> 00:00:50.962
- [Narrator] These guys
are experts in finding

00:00:50.962 --> 00:00:53.682
real world lessons in existing games.

00:00:53.682 --> 00:00:55.849
Games like Clash of Clans.

00:00:57.101 --> 00:00:59.359
And they're using these
lessons to turn game play

00:00:59.359 --> 00:01:01.378
into a universal learning
tool through a theory

00:01:01.378 --> 00:01:03.760
called Constructivism.

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:05.198
- Constructivism basically says you need

00:01:05.198 --> 00:01:08.445
to be put in situations that allow you

00:01:08.445 --> 00:01:12.146
to construct your
understanding of a particular

00:01:12.146 --> 00:01:14.044
topic or phenomenon.

00:01:14.044 --> 00:01:15.460
- We're constructing.

00:01:15.460 --> 00:01:18.677
We're making up what we
know the world to be.

00:01:18.677 --> 00:01:20.449
That's kinda constantly changing.

00:01:20.449 --> 00:01:22.370
We're adding new things into our paradigm

00:01:22.370 --> 00:01:24.403
of what we think the world
is, and that's really

00:01:24.403 --> 00:01:27.326
what education's all about.

00:01:27.326 --> 00:01:29.587
- [Broadcaster] Here is a
third grade on a field trip

00:01:29.587 --> 00:01:34.207
to get first hand knowledge
of their questions in science.

00:01:34.207 --> 00:01:37.171
- If we have play pulled into all of that.

00:01:37.171 --> 00:01:41.089
If we use games to be
able to learn in that way,

00:01:41.089 --> 00:01:44.735
that's that inherently
interesting, inherently fun

00:01:44.735 --> 00:01:46.440
way to do that, but
they don't even realize

00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:48.145
that they're learning when they are.

00:01:48.145 --> 00:01:50.118
- That can be true of video games as well,

00:01:50.118 --> 00:01:52.407
depending on the type of video game.

00:01:52.407 --> 00:01:54.333
Games that you really want to start with

00:01:54.333 --> 00:01:57.287
that have the high ROI
are cooperative games.

00:01:57.287 --> 00:01:59.617
In a cooperative game, the game itself is

00:01:59.617 --> 00:02:02.974
trying to defeat you, and
you do need to work together,

00:02:02.974 --> 00:02:04.296
and coordinate your efforts.

00:02:04.296 --> 00:02:06.341
Clash of Clans is a good
example of a cooperative game

00:02:06.341 --> 00:02:07.265
because you have a clan.

00:02:07.265 --> 00:02:09.881
You want your other clan mates to do well.

00:02:09.881 --> 00:02:11.681
All boats rise in Clash.

00:02:11.681 --> 00:02:13.284
As you play some of
these games, you see the

00:02:13.284 --> 00:02:15.410
very specific skills and competencies

00:02:15.410 --> 00:02:17.807
that can be developed
if you leverage the game

00:02:17.807 --> 00:02:20.287
intentionally in that direction.

00:02:20.287 --> 00:02:24.037
- Clash of Clans was
unavoidable in my class.

00:02:24.883 --> 00:02:26.608
And of course, as a
teacher I started to think

00:02:26.608 --> 00:02:28.858
about how could that play into my lesson.

00:02:28.858 --> 00:02:31.026
And so I started to create sort of like

00:02:31.026 --> 00:02:32.153
a review game.

00:02:32.153 --> 00:02:34.027
And so whenever they would
answer questions correctly

00:02:34.027 --> 00:02:35.774
the would earn points.

00:02:35.774 --> 00:02:37.335
But the thing that came
from Clash of Clans

00:02:37.335 --> 00:02:40.573
was they built up those points
to be able to buy things.

00:02:40.573 --> 00:02:42.774
And, they loved it.

00:02:42.774 --> 00:02:45.574
- It's an investment
portfolio, and it supports

00:02:45.574 --> 00:02:49.383
your choice making in terms
of long term investment

00:02:49.383 --> 00:02:51.745
and savings, short term
investment, and that's not

00:02:51.745 --> 00:02:52.578
just for kids.

00:02:52.578 --> 00:02:55.271
You see it with kids, but
you also see it with adults.

00:02:55.271 --> 00:02:57.159
Adults can get better
at this stuff by playing

00:02:57.159 --> 00:02:58.794
these types of games.

00:02:58.794 --> 00:03:01.062
So when you break it down
to individual mechanics,

00:03:01.062 --> 00:03:03.017
you think about in terms of resources.

00:03:03.017 --> 00:03:04.577
What are the resources available to me.

00:03:04.577 --> 00:03:07.898
So Clash has elixir,
dark elixir, and gold.

00:03:07.898 --> 00:03:11.478
- Clash is really good
at presenting choices for

00:03:11.478 --> 00:03:15.416
both immediate gain, and delayed gain.

00:03:15.416 --> 00:03:17.185
- So there is that famous
marshmallow experiment

00:03:17.185 --> 00:03:19.570
where you put a marshmallow
in front of a kid

00:03:19.570 --> 00:03:21.883
and say, okay, you can
eat the marshmallow now,

00:03:21.883 --> 00:03:23.167
or you can wait.

00:03:23.167 --> 00:03:25.068
And if you keep that
marshmallow in ten minutes

00:03:25.068 --> 00:03:26.492
I'll give you another
marshmallow, and you'll

00:03:26.492 --> 00:03:27.724
have two instead of one.

00:03:27.724 --> 00:03:29.972
Some kids are able to
delay the gratification

00:03:29.972 --> 00:03:31.567
and say, okay, I'm gonna wait for that

00:03:31.567 --> 00:03:32.427
second marshmallow.

00:03:32.427 --> 00:03:33.260
That's great.

00:03:33.260 --> 00:03:35.764
Others are like, I'm
eating the marshmallow.

00:03:35.764 --> 00:03:37.797
- Oh, I love the marshmallow experiment.

00:03:37.797 --> 00:03:39.797
You know, delayed
gratification in our society

00:03:39.797 --> 00:03:42.783
is something that all of
us really struggle with.

00:03:42.783 --> 00:03:45.182
If you know why you're playing it, and you

00:03:45.182 --> 00:03:47.135
know what you're getting out of it,

00:03:47.135 --> 00:03:49.464
then sometimes you can learn lessons

00:03:49.464 --> 00:03:51.308
by playing video games
that you can't learn

00:03:51.308 --> 00:03:52.730
a lot of other ways.

00:03:52.730 --> 00:03:54.792
- There is so much
available right now that

00:03:54.792 --> 00:03:57.789
you can pull off the shelf or download and

00:03:57.789 --> 00:04:00.354
develop some ideas about
how that connects to

00:04:00.354 --> 00:04:01.520
skills in the workplace.

00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:03.727
You don't have to go all the way.

00:04:03.727 --> 00:04:05.361
- [Broadcaster] The chance
to play is essential

00:04:05.361 --> 00:04:09.402
to the wellbeing of
all, regardless of age.

00:04:09.402 --> 00:04:11.844
- Surely the games may change over time,

00:04:11.844 --> 00:04:14.942
but we in our desire to play together and

00:04:14.942 --> 00:04:17.708
have immersive experiences
in being community; we've

00:04:17.708 --> 00:04:19.855
been doing that forever.

00:04:19.855 --> 00:04:22.370
- Having people play
cooperative games where

00:04:22.370 --> 00:04:25.085
it enhances their communication,
enhances leadership.

00:04:25.085 --> 00:04:29.718
Cooperative games are a rich
vein to mine in that regard.

00:04:29.718 --> 00:04:32.229
- In a way, more effective
than a trust fall.

00:04:32.229 --> 00:04:33.229
- Yes. Yeah.

